1
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:08,319
It was in the fourth year of my apprenticeship to Joe, and it was

2
00:00:08,319 --> 00:00:14,000
a Saturday night. There was a group assembled round the fire at the Three Jolly Bargemen,

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,039
attentive to Mr. Wopsle as he read the newspaper aloud. Of that group I was one.

4
00:00:21,039 --> 00:00:27,840
A highly popular murder had been committed, and Mr. Wopsle was imbrued in blood to the eyebrows.

5
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:33,400
He gloated over every abhorrent adjective in the description, and identified himself with

6
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:39,519
every witness at the Inquest. He faintly moaned, “I am done for,” as the victim,

7
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:46,199
and he barbarously bellowed, “I’ll serve you out,” as the murderer. He gave the medical testimony, in

8
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:52,798
pointed imitation of our local practitioner; and he piped and shook, as the aged turnpike-keeper

9
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:58,039
who had heard blows, to an extent so very paralytic as to suggest a doubt regarding the

10
00:00:58,039 --> 00:01:05,798
mental competency of that witness. The coroner, in Mr. Wopsle’s hands, became Timon of Athens;

11
00:01:05,798 --> 00:01:12,520
the beadle, Coriolanus. He enjoyed himself thoroughly, and we all enjoyed ourselves, and

12
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:20,638
were delightfully comfortable. In this cosey state of mind we came to the verdict Wilful Murder.

13
00:01:20,638 --> 00:01:25,680
Then, and not sooner, I became aware of a strange gentleman leaning over the back of

14
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:32,000
the settle opposite me, looking on. There was an expression of contempt on his face,

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,400
and he bit the side of a great forefinger as he watched the group of faces.

16
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,958
“Well!” said the stranger to Mr. Wopsle, when the reading was done,

17
00:01:41,479 --> 00:01:45,919
“you have settled it all to your own satisfaction, I have no doubt?”

18
00:01:45,919 --> 00:01:48,199
Everybody started and looked up,

19
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:53,519
as if it were the murderer. He looked at everybody coldly and sarcastically.

20
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:57,958
“Guilty, of course?” said he. “Out with it. Come!”

21
00:01:57,958 --> 00:02:02,359
“Sir,” returned Mr. Wopsle, “without having the honour of your acquaintance,

22
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:09,039
I do say Guilty.” Upon this we all took courage to unite in a confirmatory murmur.

23
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:10,919
“I know you do,” said the stranger;

24
00:02:11,559 --> 00:02:17,879
“I knew you would. I told you so. But now I’ll ask you a question. Do you know, or do you not know,

25
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:26,638
that the law of England supposes every man to be innocent, until he is proved—proved—to be guilty?”

26
00:02:26,638 --> 00:02:33,000
“Sir,” Mr. Wopsle began to reply, “as an Englishman myself, I—”

27
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,759
“Come!” said the stranger, biting his forefinger at him. “Don’t evade the

28
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:43,438
question. Either you know it, or you don’t know it. Which is it to be?”

29
00:02:43,438 --> 00:02:48,919
He stood with his head on one side and himself on one side, in a bullying, interrogative manner,

30
00:02:48,919 --> 00:02:54,638
and he threw his forefinger at Mr. Wopsle,—as it were to mark him out—before biting it again.

31
00:02:54,638 --> 00:02:58,680
“Now!” said he. “Do you know it, or don’t you know it?”

32
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,879
“Certainly I know it,” replied Mr. Wopsle.

33
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:06,000
“Certainly you know it. Then why didn’t you say so at first? Now,

34
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,359
I’ll ask you another question,”—taking possession of Mr. Wopsle, as if he had

35
00:03:10,359 --> 00:03:15,879
a right to him,—“do you know that none of these witnesses have yet been cross-examined?”

36
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:21,039
Mr. Wopsle was beginning, “I can only say—” when the stranger stopped him.

37
00:03:21,039 --> 00:03:27,159
“What? You won’t answer the question, yes or no? Now, I’ll try you again.” Throwing

38
00:03:27,159 --> 00:03:33,118
his finger at him again. “Attend to me. Are you aware, or are you not aware, that none of these

39
00:03:33,118 --> 00:03:41,080
witnesses have yet been cross-examined? Come, I only want one word from you. Yes, or no?”

40
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:46,438
Mr. Wopsle hesitated, and we all began to conceive rather a poor opinion of him.

41
00:03:46,438 --> 00:03:50,159
“Come!” said the stranger, “I’ll help you. You don’t deserve help,

42
00:03:50,159 --> 00:03:55,719
but I’ll help you. Look at that paper you hold in your hand. What is it?”

43
00:03:55,719 --> 00:04:00,559
“What is it?” repeated Mr. Wopsle, eyeing it, much at a loss.

44
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:05,319
“Is it,” pursued the stranger in his most sarcastic and suspicious manner,

45
00:04:05,319 --> 00:04:08,199
“the printed paper you have just been reading from?”

46
00:04:08,199 --> 00:04:08,958
“Undoubtedly.”

47
00:04:08,958 --> 00:04:16,039
“Undoubtedly. Now, turn to that paper, and tell me whether it distinctly states that the

48
00:04:16,039 --> 00:04:22,278
prisoner expressly said that his legal advisers instructed him altogether to reserve his defence?”

49
00:04:22,278 --> 00:04:25,919
“I read that just now,” Mr. Wopsle pleaded.

50
00:04:25,919 --> 00:04:31,118
“Never mind what you read just now, sir; I don’t ask you what you read just now. You may read

51
00:04:31,118 --> 00:04:36,159
the Lord’s Prayer backwards, if you like,—and, perhaps, have done it before to-day. Turn to the

52
00:04:36,159 --> 00:04:42,400
paper. No, no, no my friend; not to the top of the column; you know better than that; to the bottom,

53
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:50,558
to the bottom.” (We all began to think Mr. Wopsle full of subterfuge.) “Well? Have you found it?”

54
00:04:50,559 --> 00:04:53,159
“Here it is,” said Mr. Wopsle.

55
00:04:53,159 --> 00:04:57,798
“Now, follow that passage with your eye, and tell me whether it distinctly states

56
00:04:57,798 --> 00:05:02,080
that the prisoner expressly said that he was instructed by his legal advisers

57
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:07,438
wholly to reserve his defence? Come! Do you make that of it?”

58
00:05:07,438 --> 00:05:10,718
Mr. Wopsle answered, “Those are not the exact words.”

59
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:19,438
“Not the exact words!” repeated the gentleman bitterly. “Is that the exact substance?”

60
00:05:19,438 --> 00:05:22,479
“Yes,” said Mr. Wopsle.

61
00:05:22,479 --> 00:05:26,878
“Yes,” repeated the stranger, looking round at the rest of the company with

62
00:05:26,879 --> 00:05:32,438
his right hand extended towards the witness, Wopsle. “And now I ask you

63
00:05:32,438 --> 00:05:37,319
what you say to the conscience of that man who, with that passage before his eyes,

64
00:05:37,319 --> 00:05:45,118
can lay his head upon his pillow after having pronounced a fellow-creature guilty, unheard?”

65
00:05:45,118 --> 00:05:49,400
We all began to suspect that Mr. Wopsle was not the man we had thought him,

66
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:54,838
and that he was beginning to be found out. “And that same man, remember,” pursued the

67
00:05:54,840 --> 00:06:00,000
gentleman, throwing his finger at Mr. Wopsle heavily,—“that same man might be summoned as

68
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,359
a juryman upon this very trial, and, having thus deeply committed himself, might return

69
00:06:05,359 --> 00:06:10,879
to the bosom of his family and lay his head upon his pillow, after deliberately swearing

70
00:06:10,879 --> 00:06:14,840
that he would well and truly try the issue joined between Our Sovereign Lord the King and

71
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:23,039
the prisoner at the bar, and would a true verdict give according to the evidence, so help him God!”

72
00:06:23,039 --> 00:06:27,680
We were all deeply persuaded that the unfortunate Wopsle had gone too far,

73
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:32,039
and had better stop in his reckless career while there was yet time.

74
00:06:32,039 --> 00:06:36,000
The strange gentleman, with an air of authority not to be disputed,

75
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,879
and with a manner expressive of knowing something secret about every one of us that

76
00:06:39,879 --> 00:06:46,680
would effectually do for each individual if he chose to disclose it, left the back of the settle,

77
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:53,319
and came into the space between the two settles, in front of the fire, where he remained standing,

78
00:06:53,319 --> 00:06:58,637
his left hand in his pocket, and he biting the forefinger of his right.

79
00:06:58,638 --> 00:07:02,559
“From information I have received,” said he, looking round at us as we

80
00:07:02,559 --> 00:07:08,159
all quailed before him, “I have reason to believe there is a blacksmith among you,

81
00:07:08,159 --> 00:07:14,359
by name Joseph—or Joe—Gargery. Which is the man?”

82
00:07:14,359 --> 00:07:17,199
“Here is the man,” said Joe.

83
00:07:17,199 --> 00:07:21,958
The strange gentleman beckoned him out of his place, and Joe went.

84
00:07:21,958 --> 00:07:28,118
“You have an apprentice,” pursued the stranger, “commonly known as Pip? Is he here?”

85
00:07:28,118 --> 00:07:30,719
“I am here!” I cried.

86
00:07:30,719 --> 00:07:35,680
The stranger did not recognise me, but I recognised him as the gentleman I had met

87
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:41,039
on the stairs, on the occasion of my second visit to Miss Havisham. I had known him the

88
00:07:41,039 --> 00:07:45,438
moment I saw him looking over the settle, and now that I stood confronting him with

89
00:07:45,438 --> 00:07:50,319
his hand upon my shoulder, I checked off again in detail his large head,

90
00:07:50,319 --> 00:07:57,637
his dark complexion, his deep-set eyes, his bushy black eyebrows, his large watch-chain,

91
00:07:57,638 --> 00:08:05,000
his strong black dots of beard and whisker, and even the smell of scented soap on his great hand.

92
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,559
“I wish to have a private conference with you two,” said he, when he had surveyed me at his

93
00:08:09,559 --> 00:08:15,159
leisure. “It will take a little time. Perhaps we had better go to your place

94
00:08:15,159 --> 00:08:21,680
of residence. I prefer not to anticipate my communication here; you will impart as much or

95
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:28,159
as little of it as you please to your friends afterwards; I have nothing to do with that.”

96
00:08:28,159 --> 00:08:32,919
Amidst a wondering silence, we three walked out of the Jolly Bargemen, and in a wondering

97
00:08:32,919 --> 00:08:38,080
silence walked home. While going along, the strange gentleman occasionally looked at me,

98
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:43,919
and occasionally bit the side of his finger. As we neared home, Joe vaguely acknowledging

99
00:08:43,919 --> 00:08:50,638
the occasion as an impressive and ceremonious one, went on ahead to open the front door. Our

100
00:08:50,638 --> 00:08:56,278
conference was held in the state parlour, which was feebly lighted by one candle.

101
00:08:56,278 --> 00:09:01,118
It began with the strange gentleman’s sitting down at the table, drawing the candle to him,

102
00:09:01,118 --> 00:09:04,879
and looking over some entries in his pocket-book. He then put up

103
00:09:04,879 --> 00:09:07,438
the pocket-book and set the candle a little aside,

104
00:09:07,438 --> 00:09:13,519
after peering round it into the darkness at Joe and me, to ascertain which was which.

105
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:20,719
“My name,” he said, “is Jaggers, and I am a lawyer in London. I am pretty well known. I have unusual

106
00:09:20,719 --> 00:09:26,798
business to transact with you, and I commence by explaining that it is not of my originating. If

107
00:09:26,798 --> 00:09:33,039
my advice had been asked, I should not have been here. It was not asked, and you see me

108
00:09:33,039 --> 00:09:40,438
here. What I have to do as the confidential agent of another, I do. No less, no more.”

109
00:09:42,118 --> 00:09:45,199
Finding that he could not see us very well from where he sat,

110
00:09:45,199 --> 00:09:50,359
he got up, and threw one leg over the back of a chair and leaned upon it;

111
00:09:50,359 --> 00:09:55,679
thus having one foot on the seat of the chair, and one foot on the ground.

112
00:09:55,680 --> 00:10:02,000
“Now, Joseph Gargery, I am the bearer of an offer to relieve you of this

113
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,159
young fellow your apprentice. You would not object to cancel his indentures at

114
00:10:07,159 --> 00:10:13,000
his request and for his good? You would want nothing for so doing?”

115
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,798
“Lord forbid that I should want anything for not standing in Pip’s way,” said Joe, staring.

116
00:10:17,798 --> 00:10:22,278
“Lord forbidding is pious, but not to the purpose,” returned Mr. Jaggers.

117
00:10:22,278 --> 00:10:26,519
“The question is, Would you want anything? Do you want anything?”

118
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:31,080
“The answer is,” returned Joe, sternly, “No.”

119
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:36,879
I thought Mr. Jaggers glanced at Joe, as if he considered him a fool for his disinterestedness.

120
00:10:36,879 --> 00:10:42,638
But I was too much bewildered between breathless curiosity and surprise, to be sure of it.

121
00:10:42,638 --> 00:10:46,278
“Very well,” said Mr. Jaggers. “Recollect the admission you

122
00:10:46,278 --> 00:10:49,879
have made, and don’t try to go from it presently.”

123
00:10:49,879 --> 00:10:52,520
“Who’s a-going to try?” retorted Joe.

124
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:56,159
“I don’t say anybody is. Do you keep a dog?”

125
00:10:56,159 --> 00:11:00,719
“Yes, I do keep a dog.” “Bear in mind then, that Brag

126
00:11:00,719 --> 00:11:06,958
is a good dog, but Holdfast is a better. Bear that in mind, will you?” repeated Mr. Jaggers,

127
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:12,798
shutting his eyes and nodding his head at Joe, as if he were forgiving him something. “Now,

128
00:11:12,798 --> 00:11:17,599
I return to this young fellow. And the communication I have got to make is,

129
00:11:17,599 --> 00:11:24,199
that he has great expectations.” Joe and I gasped, and looked at one another.

130
00:11:24,199 --> 00:11:30,080
“I am instructed to communicate to him,” said Mr. Jaggers, throwing his finger at me sideways,

131
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:35,278
“that he will come into a handsome property. Further, that it is the desire of the present

132
00:11:35,278 --> 00:11:40,637
possessor of that property, that he be immediately removed from his present sphere of life and from

133
00:11:40,638 --> 00:11:47,958
this place, and be brought up as a gentleman,—in a word, as a young fellow of great expectations.”

134
00:11:47,958 --> 00:11:52,638
My dream was out; my wild fancy was surpassed by sober reality;

135
00:11:52,638 --> 00:11:57,520
Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune on a grand scale.

136
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:01,719
“Now, Mr. Pip,” pursued the lawyer, “I address the rest of what I have to say,

137
00:12:01,719 --> 00:12:06,958
to you. You are to understand, first, that it is the request of the person from

138
00:12:06,958 --> 00:12:10,240
whom I take my instructions that you always bear the name of Pip.

139
00:12:11,958 --> 00:12:15,919
You will have no objection, I dare say, to your great expectations being encumbered

140
00:12:15,919 --> 00:12:23,159
with that easy condition. But if you have any objection, this is the time to mention it.”

141
00:12:23,159 --> 00:12:27,558
My heart was beating so fast, and there was such a singing in my ears,

142
00:12:27,559 --> 00:12:31,039
that I could scarcely stammer I had no objection.

143
00:12:31,039 --> 00:12:36,958
“I should think not! Now you are to understand, secondly, Mr. Pip, that the name of the person

144
00:12:36,958 --> 00:12:41,278
who is your liberal benefactor remains a profound secret, until the person chooses

145
00:12:41,278 --> 00:12:46,080
to reveal it. I am empowered to mention that it is the intention of the person to reveal

146
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:52,680
it at first hand by word of mouth to yourself. When or where that intention may be carried out,

147
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:59,958
I cannot say; no one can say. It may be years hence. Now, you are distinctly to understand

148
00:12:59,958 --> 00:13:03,958
that you are most positively prohibited from making any inquiry on this head,

149
00:13:03,958 --> 00:13:10,199
or any allusion or reference, however distant, to any individual whomsoever as the individual,

150
00:13:10,199 --> 00:13:16,118
in all the communications you may have with me. If you have a suspicion in your own breast,

151
00:13:16,118 --> 00:13:21,598
keep that suspicion in your own breast. It is not the least to the purpose what the reasons of this

152
00:13:21,599 --> 00:13:27,400
prohibition are; they may be the strongest and gravest reasons, or they may be mere

153
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:35,118
whim. This is not for you to inquire into. The condition is laid down. Your acceptance of it,

154
00:13:35,118 --> 00:13:40,199
and your observance of it as binding, is the only remaining condition that I am charged with,

155
00:13:40,199 --> 00:13:47,000
by the person from whom I take my instructions, and for whom I am not otherwise responsible. That

156
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:52,798
person is the person from whom you derive your expectations, and the secret is solely held by

157
00:13:52,798 --> 00:13:59,000
that person and by me. Again, not a very difficult condition with which to encumber such a rise in

158
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:06,359
fortune; but if you have any objection to it, this is the time to mention it. Speak out.”

159
00:14:06,359 --> 00:14:10,359
Once more, I stammered with difficulty that I had no objection.

160
00:14:10,359 --> 00:14:16,797
“I should think not! Now, Mr. Pip, I have done with stipulations.” Though he called me Mr. Pip,

161
00:14:16,798 --> 00:14:21,919
and began rather to make up to me, he still could not get rid of a certain air of bullying

162
00:14:21,919 --> 00:14:27,798
suspicion; and even now he occasionally shut his eyes and threw his finger at me while he spoke,

163
00:14:27,798 --> 00:14:32,479
as much as to express that he knew all kinds of things to my disparagement, if he only

164
00:14:32,479 --> 00:14:38,718
chose to mention them. “We come next, to mere details of arrangement. You must know that,

165
00:14:38,719 --> 00:14:44,199
although I have used the term ‘expectations’ more than once, you are not endowed with expectations

166
00:14:44,199 --> 00:14:50,598
only. There is already lodged in my hands a sum of money amply sufficient for your suitable education

167
00:14:50,599 --> 00:14:59,400
and maintenance. You will please consider me your guardian. Oh!” for I was going to thank him,

168
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:05,718
“I tell you at once, I am paid for my services, or I shouldn’t render them. It is considered

169
00:15:05,719 --> 00:15:10,039
that you must be better educated, in accordance with your altered position,

170
00:15:10,719 --> 00:15:17,559
and that you will be alive to the importance and necessity of at once entering on that advantage.”

171
00:15:17,559 --> 00:15:20,798
I said I had always longed for it.

172
00:15:20,798 --> 00:15:24,879
“Never mind what you have always longed for, Mr. Pip,” he retorted;

173
00:15:24,879 --> 00:15:30,520
“keep to the record. If you long for it now, that’s enough. Am I answered

174
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:35,919
that you are ready to be placed at once under some proper tutor? Is that it?”

175
00:15:35,919 --> 00:15:39,400
I stammered yes, that was it.

176
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:45,118
“Good. Now, your inclinations are to be consulted. I don’t think that wise, mind,

177
00:15:45,118 --> 00:15:50,598
but it’s my trust. Have you ever heard of any tutor whom you would prefer to another?”

178
00:15:50,599 --> 00:15:54,958
I had never heard of any tutor but Biddy and Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt;

179
00:15:54,958 --> 00:15:58,319
so, I replied in the negative.

180
00:15:58,319 --> 00:16:02,758
“There is a certain tutor, of whom I have some knowledge, who I think might suit the

181
00:16:02,759 --> 00:16:10,438
purpose,” said Mr. Jaggers. “I don’t recommend him, observe; because I never recommend anybody.

182
00:16:10,438 --> 00:16:14,278
The gentleman I speak of is one Mr. Matthew Pocket.”

183
00:16:14,278 --> 00:16:22,000
Ah! I caught at the name directly. Miss Havisham’s relation. The Matthew whom Mr. and Mrs. Camilla

184
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:28,359
had spoken of. The Matthew whose place was to be at Miss Havisham’s head, when she lay dead,

185
00:16:28,359 --> 00:16:35,199
in her bride’s dress on the bride’s table. “You know the name?” said Mr. Jaggers,

186
00:16:35,199 --> 00:16:39,958
looking shrewdly at me, and then shutting up his eyes while he waited for my answer.

187
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:44,039
My answer was, that I had heard of the name.

188
00:16:44,039 --> 00:16:51,638
“Oh!” said he. “You have heard of the name. But the question is, what do you say of it?”

189
00:16:51,638 --> 00:16:57,638
I said, or tried to say, that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—

190
00:16:57,638 --> 00:17:05,438
“No, my young friend!” he interrupted, shaking his great head very slowly. “Recollect yourself!”

191
00:17:05,438 --> 00:17:12,000
Not recollecting myself, I began again that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—

192
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:17,240
“No, my young friend,” he interrupted, shaking his head and frowning and smiling both at once,—“no,

193
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:21,278
no, no; it’s very well done, but it won’t do; you are too young

194
00:17:21,278 --> 00:17:27,718
to fix me with it. Recommendation is not the word, Mr. Pip. Try another.”

195
00:17:27,719 --> 00:17:33,719
Correcting myself, I said that I was much obliged to him for his mention of Mr. Matthew Pocket—

196
00:17:33,719 --> 00:17:39,919
“That’s more like it!” cried Mr. Jaggers.—And (I added), I would gladly try that gentleman.

197
00:17:41,039 --> 00:17:45,519
“Good. You had better try him in his own house. The way shall be prepared for you,

198
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:50,798
and you can see his son first, who is in London. When will you come to London?”

199
00:17:50,798 --> 00:17:54,520
I said (glancing at Joe, who stood looking on, motionless),

200
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:57,638
that I supposed I could come directly.

201
00:17:57,638 --> 00:18:03,118
“First,” said Mr. Jaggers, “you should have some new clothes to come in, and they should not be

202
00:18:03,118 --> 00:18:09,840
working-clothes. Say this day week. You’ll want some money. Shall I leave you twenty guineas?”

203
00:18:10,879 --> 00:18:15,919
He produced a long purse, with the greatest coolness, and counted them out on the table

204
00:18:15,919 --> 00:18:22,599
and pushed them over to me. This was the first time he had taken his leg from the chair. He sat

205
00:18:22,599 --> 00:18:28,958
astride of the chair when he had pushed the money over, and sat swinging his purse and eyeing Joe.

206
00:18:28,958 --> 00:18:33,000
“Well, Joseph Gargery? You look dumbfoundered?”

207
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:37,240
“I am!” said Joe, in a very decided manner.

208
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:39,840
“It was understood that you wanted nothing for yourself, remember?”

209
00:18:41,438 --> 00:18:44,039
“It were understood,” said Joe. “And it

210
00:18:44,039 --> 00:18:49,000
are understood. And it ever will be similar according.”

211
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,559
“But what,” said Mr. Jaggers, swinging his purse,—“what if

212
00:18:52,559 --> 00:18:56,559
it was in my instructions to make you a present, as compensation?”

213
00:18:56,559 --> 00:18:59,719
“As compensation what for?” Joe demanded.

214
00:18:59,719 --> 00:19:02,438
“For the loss of his services.”

215
00:19:02,438 --> 00:19:08,080
Joe laid his hand upon my shoulder with the touch of a woman. I have often thought him since,

216
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:13,919
like the steam-hammer that can crush a man or pat an egg-shell, in his combination of strength with

217
00:19:13,919 --> 00:19:20,680
gentleness. “Pip is that hearty welcome,” said Joe, “to go free with his services,

218
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:26,400
to honour and fortun’, as no words can tell him. But if you think as Money can make compensation to

219
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:32,240
me for the loss of the little child—what come to the forge—and ever the best of friends!—”

220
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:39,240
O dear good Joe, whom I was so ready to leave and so unthankful to, I see you again,

221
00:19:39,240 --> 00:19:44,598
with your muscular blacksmith’s arm before your eyes, and your broad chest heaving,

222
00:19:44,599 --> 00:19:52,278
and your voice dying away. O dear good faithful tender Joe, I feel the loving tremble of your

223
00:19:52,278 --> 00:19:58,319
hand upon my arm, as solemnly this day as if it had been the rustle of an angel’s wing!

224
00:19:58,319 --> 00:20:04,159
But I encouraged Joe at the time. I was lost in the mazes of my future fortunes, and could not

225
00:20:04,159 --> 00:20:10,598
retrace the by-paths we had trodden together. I begged Joe to be comforted, for (as he said)

226
00:20:10,599 --> 00:20:17,919
we had ever been the best of friends, and (as I said) we ever would be so. Joe scooped his eyes

227
00:20:17,919 --> 00:20:25,240
with his disengaged wrist, as if he were bent on gouging himself, but said not another word.

228
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:31,159
Mr. Jaggers had looked on at this, as one who recognised in Joe the village idiot, and in me

229
00:20:31,159 --> 00:20:37,958
his keeper. When it was over, he said, weighing in his hand the purse he had ceased to swing:—

230
00:20:37,958 --> 00:20:43,798
“Now, Joseph Gargery, I warn you this is your last chance. No half measures

231
00:20:43,798 --> 00:20:48,159
with me. If you mean to take a present that I have it in charge to make you, speak out,

232
00:20:48,159 --> 00:20:55,359
and you shall have it. If on the contrary you mean to say—” Here, to his great amazement, he was

233
00:20:55,359 --> 00:21:02,080
stopped by Joe’s suddenly working round him with every demonstration of a fell pugilistic purpose.

234
00:21:02,080 --> 00:21:06,359
“Which I meantersay,” cried Joe, “that if you come into my place bull-baiting

235
00:21:06,359 --> 00:21:10,199
and badgering me, come out! Which I meantersay as sech if you’re a man,

236
00:21:10,199 --> 00:21:15,598
come on! Which I meantersay that what I say, I meantersay and stand or fall by!”

237
00:21:15,599 --> 00:21:20,638
I drew Joe away, and he immediately became placable; merely stating to me, in an obliging

238
00:21:20,638 --> 00:21:25,879
manner and as a polite expostulatory notice to any one whom it might happen to concern,

239
00:21:25,879 --> 00:21:31,520
that he were not a-going to be bull-baited and badgered in his own place. Mr. Jaggers

240
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:38,400
had risen when Joe demonstrated, and had backed near the door. Without evincing any inclination

241
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:45,919
to come in again, he there delivered his valedictory remarks. They were these.

242
00:21:45,919 --> 00:21:52,479
“Well, Mr. Pip, I think the sooner you leave here—as you are to be a gentleman—the better.

243
00:21:52,479 --> 00:21:56,118
Let it stand for this day week, and you shall receive my printed address in the

244
00:21:56,118 --> 00:22:00,319
meantime. You can take a hackney-coach at the stage-coach office in London,

245
00:22:00,319 --> 00:22:05,000
and come straight to me. Understand, that I express no opinion, one way or other,

246
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:09,638
on the trust I undertake. I am paid for undertaking it, and I do so.

247
00:22:10,359 --> 00:22:18,558
Now, understand that, finally. Understand that!” He was throwing his finger at both of us,

248
00:22:18,559 --> 00:22:24,638
and I think would have gone on, but for his seeming to think Joe dangerous, and going off.

249
00:22:24,638 --> 00:22:27,759
Something came into my head which induced me to run after him, as he

250
00:22:27,759 --> 00:22:33,638
was going down to the Jolly Bargemen, where he had left a hired carriage.

251
00:22:33,638 --> 00:22:36,359
“I beg your pardon, Mr. Jaggers.”

252
00:22:36,359 --> 00:22:39,679
“Halloa!” said he, facing round, “what’s the matter?”

253
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:42,278
“I wish to be quite right, Mr. Jaggers,

254
00:22:42,278 --> 00:22:46,840
and to keep to your directions; so I thought I had better ask. Would there

255
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:52,400
be any objection to my taking leave of any one I know, about here, before I go away?”

256
00:22:52,400 --> 00:22:56,359
“No,” said he, looking as if he hardly understood me.

257
00:22:56,359 --> 00:23:00,080
“I don’t mean in the village only, but up town?”

258
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:03,680
“No,” said he. “No objection.”

259
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:08,798
I thanked him and ran home again, and there I found that Joe had already locked the front door

260
00:23:08,798 --> 00:23:14,278
and vacated the state parlour, and was seated by the kitchen fire with a hand on each knee,

261
00:23:14,278 --> 00:23:19,000
gazing intently at the burning coals. I too sat down before the

262
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:24,080
fire and gazed at the coals, and nothing was said for a long time.

263
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:28,519
My sister was in her cushioned chair in her corner, and Biddy sat at her needle-work

264
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:35,520
before the fire, and Joe sat next Biddy, and I sat next Joe in the corner opposite my sister.

265
00:23:35,520 --> 00:23:41,039
The more I looked into the glowing coals, the more incapable I became of looking at Joe;

266
00:23:41,039 --> 00:23:46,159
the longer the silence lasted, the more unable I felt to speak.

267
00:23:46,159 --> 00:23:51,438
At length I got out, “Joe, have you told Biddy?”

268
00:23:51,438 --> 00:23:56,797
“No, Pip,” returned Joe, still looking at the fire, and holding his knees tight, as if he

269
00:23:56,798 --> 00:24:03,479
had private information that they intended to make off somewhere, “which I left it to yourself, Pip.”

270
00:24:03,479 --> 00:24:05,919
“I would rather you told, Joe.”

271
00:24:05,919 --> 00:24:10,759
“Pip’s a gentleman of fortun’ then,” said Joe, “and God bless him in it!”

272
00:24:10,759 --> 00:24:15,359
Biddy dropped her work, and looked at me. Joe held his knees and looked at me. I

273
00:24:15,359 --> 00:24:21,879
looked at both of them. After a pause, they both heartily congratulated me;

274
00:24:21,879 --> 00:24:27,798
but there was a certain touch of sadness in their congratulations that I rather resented.

275
00:24:27,798 --> 00:24:33,319
I took it upon myself to impress Biddy (and through Biddy, Joe) with the grave obligation

276
00:24:33,319 --> 00:24:39,879
I considered my friends under, to know nothing and say nothing about the maker of my fortune.

277
00:24:39,879 --> 00:24:44,680
It would all come out in good time, I observed, and in the meanwhile nothing was to be said,

278
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:47,958
save that I had come into great expectations from a mysterious

279
00:24:47,958 --> 00:24:54,080
patron. Biddy nodded her head thoughtfully at the fire as she took up her work again,

280
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:59,919
and said she would be very particular; and Joe, still detaining his knees, said, “Ay,

281
00:24:59,919 --> 00:25:06,278
ay, I’ll be ekervally partickler, Pip;” and then they congratulated me again, and went on

282
00:25:06,278 --> 00:25:13,438
to express so much wonder at the notion of my being a gentleman that I didn’t half like it.

283
00:25:13,438 --> 00:25:17,797
Infinite pains were then taken by Biddy to convey to my sister some

284
00:25:17,798 --> 00:25:24,278
idea of what had happened. To the best of my belief, those efforts entirely failed.

285
00:25:24,278 --> 00:25:29,319
She laughed and nodded her head a great many times, and even repeated after Biddy,

286
00:25:29,319 --> 00:25:35,319
the words “Pip” and “Property.” But I doubt if they had more meaning in them than an

287
00:25:35,319 --> 00:25:41,039
election cry, and I cannot suggest a darker picture of her state of mind.

288
00:25:41,039 --> 00:25:46,879
I never could have believed it without experience, but as Joe and Biddy became more at their cheerful

289
00:25:46,879 --> 00:25:53,479
ease again, I became quite gloomy. Dissatisfied with my fortune, of course I could not be;

290
00:25:53,479 --> 00:25:59,519
but it is possible that I may have been, without quite knowing it, dissatisfied with myself.

291
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:04,240
Anyhow, I sat with my elbow on my knee and my face upon my hand, looking into the fire,

292
00:26:04,240 --> 00:26:09,759
as those two talked about my going away, and about what they should do without me, and all that.

293
00:26:09,759 --> 00:26:14,118
And whenever I caught one of them looking at me, though never so pleasantly (and

294
00:26:14,118 --> 00:26:18,680
they often looked at me,—particularly Biddy), I felt offended: as if they

295
00:26:18,680 --> 00:26:25,599
were expressing some mistrust of me. Though Heaven knows they never did by word or sign.

296
00:26:25,599 --> 00:26:30,199
At those times I would get up and look out at the door; for our kitchen door opened

297
00:26:30,199 --> 00:26:36,438
at once upon the night, and stood open on summer evenings to air the room. The very

298
00:26:36,438 --> 00:26:41,199
stars to which I then raised my eyes, I am afraid I took to be but poor and

299
00:26:41,199 --> 00:26:48,438
humble stars for glittering on the rustic objects among which I had passed my life.

300
00:26:48,438 --> 00:26:52,679
“Saturday night,” said I, when we sat at our supper of bread and cheese and

301
00:26:52,680 --> 00:26:58,199
beer. “Five more days, and then the day before the day! They’ll soon go.”

302
00:26:58,199 --> 00:27:05,240
“Yes, Pip,” observed Joe, whose voice sounded hollow in his beer-mug. “They’ll soon go.”

303
00:27:05,240 --> 00:27:10,679
“Soon, soon go,” said Biddy. “I have been thinking, Joe,

304
00:27:10,680 --> 00:27:15,359
that when I go down town on Monday, and order my new clothes, I shall tell the tailor that

305
00:27:15,359 --> 00:27:19,838
I’ll come and put them on there, or that I’ll have them sent to Mr. Pumblechook’s.

306
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:24,798
It would be very disagreeable to be stared at by all the people here.”

307
00:27:24,798 --> 00:27:30,719
“Mr. and Mrs. Hubble might like to see you in your new gen-teel figure too, Pip,” said Joe,

308
00:27:30,719 --> 00:27:35,840
industriously cutting his bread, with his cheese on it, in the palm of his left hand,

309
00:27:35,840 --> 00:27:40,240
and glancing at my untasted supper as if he thought of the time when we used to compare

310
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:48,319
slices. “So might Wopsle. And the Jolly Bargemen might take it as a compliment.”

311
00:27:48,319 --> 00:27:52,159
“That’s just what I don’t want, Joe. They would make such a

312
00:27:52,159 --> 00:27:57,879
business of it,—such a coarse and common business,—that I couldn’t bear myself.”

313
00:27:57,879 --> 00:28:02,559
“Ah, that indeed, Pip!” said Joe. “If you couldn’t abear yourself—”

314
00:28:02,559 --> 00:28:06,520
Biddy asked me here, as she sat holding my sister’s plate,

315
00:28:06,520 --> 00:28:09,000
“Have you thought about when you’ll show yourself to Mr. Gargery,

316
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:12,919
and your sister and me? You will show yourself to us; won’t you?”

317
00:28:12,919 --> 00:28:15,798
“Biddy,” I returned with some resentment,

318
00:28:15,798 --> 00:28:19,599
“you are so exceedingly quick that it’s difficult to keep up with you.”

319
00:28:19,599 --> 00:28:23,039
(“She always were quick,” observed Joe.)

320
00:28:23,039 --> 00:28:27,039
“If you had waited another moment, Biddy, you would have heard me say that I shall bring my

321
00:28:27,039 --> 00:28:32,680
clothes here in a bundle one evening,—most likely on the evening before I go away.”

322
00:28:32,680 --> 00:28:38,240
Biddy said no more. Handsomely forgiving her, I soon exchanged an affectionate good night

323
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:44,759
with her and Joe, and went up to bed. When I got into my little room, I sat down and took

324
00:28:44,759 --> 00:28:50,958
a long look at it, as a mean little room that I should soon be parted from and raised above,

325
00:28:50,958 --> 00:28:57,278
for ever. It was furnished with fresh young remembrances too, and even at the same moment

326
00:28:57,278 --> 00:29:02,159
I fell into much the same confused division of mind between it and the better rooms to which I

327
00:29:02,159 --> 00:29:09,359
was going, as I had been in so often between the forge and Miss Havisham’s, and Biddy and Estella.

328
00:29:09,359 --> 00:29:12,797
The sun had been shining brightly all day on the roof of my attic,

329
00:29:12,798 --> 00:29:18,199
and the room was warm. As I put the window open and stood looking out,

330
00:29:18,199 --> 00:29:23,919
I saw Joe come slowly forth at the dark door, below, and take a turn or two in the air;

331
00:29:23,919 --> 00:29:30,840
and then I saw Biddy come, and bring him a pipe and light it for him. He never smoked so late,

332
00:29:30,840 --> 00:29:35,958
and it seemed to hint to me that he wanted comforting, for some reason or other.

333
00:29:35,958 --> 00:29:42,520
He presently stood at the door immediately beneath me, smoking his pipe, and Biddy stood there too,

334
00:29:42,520 --> 00:29:47,359
quietly talking to him, and I knew that they talked of me,

335
00:29:47,359 --> 00:29:53,758
for I heard my name mentioned in an endearing tone by both of them more than once. I would

336
00:29:53,759 --> 00:29:59,359
not have listened for more, if I could have heard more; so I drew away from the window,

337
00:29:59,359 --> 00:30:04,797
and sat down in my one chair by the bedside, feeling it very sorrowful and strange that

338
00:30:04,798 --> 00:30:10,719
this first night of my bright fortunes should be the loneliest I had ever known.

339
00:30:10,719 --> 00:30:16,159
Looking towards the open window, I saw light wreaths from Joe’s pipe floating there,

340
00:30:16,159 --> 00:30:21,759
and I fancied it was like a blessing from Joe,—not obtruded on me or paraded before me,

341
00:30:21,759 --> 00:30:27,759
but pervading the air we shared together. I put my light out, and crept into bed;

342
00:30:27,759 --> 00:30:33,000
and it was an uneasy bed now, and I never slept the old sound sleep in it any more.
